{"name":"The White Death","hex":"#dadded","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"},{"term":"death","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The cessation of life and all associated processes; the end of an organism's existence as an entity independent from its environment and its return to an inert, non-living state.","Execution (in the judicial sense)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/death"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Death (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"White Death is the fourth book in the NUMA Files series of books co-written by coauthors Clive Cussler and Paul Kemprecos, and published in 2003. The main character of this series is Kurt Austin. The story includes Nazis, Basque separatists, an outcast murderous tribe, the Kiolya, of Eskimos, Canadian natives in northern Quebec, environmental activists and the usual female sex interest."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/8/82/Whitedeathcover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Death_(novel)"},{"title":"White Death (film)","blocks":[{"text":"White Death is a 1936 Australian film directed by Edwin G. Bowen and starring Zane Grey as himself. He filmed it during a fishing expedition to Australia and it marked the first time he had played a leading role in a film."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Death_(film)"},{"title":"The White Death (film)","blocks":[{"text":"The White Death is a 1921 German silent drama film directed by Adolf Gärtner and starring Ellen Richter, Eduard von Winterstein and Hans Adalbert Schlettow."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_White_Death_(film)"},{"title":"Death","blocks":[{"text":"Death is the end of an organism's life. It is the irreversible cessation of biological functions that sustain a living organism. Entropy, nature's tendency toward disorder, can contribute to the breakdown of living systems over time. Upon death, organisms stop exhibiting behavior and generally begin to decompose through various physical and chemical processes. Death can occur at any stage of development, including in infancy. A person may experience clinical death that is reversible, resulting in some cases in a near-death experience. In humans, establishing death of the brain determines that an individual has irreversibly died."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg/330px-Hendrick_Andriessen_-_Vanity_Piece_-_1914-DE_-_Museum_of_Fine_Arts_Ghent_%28MSK%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Death"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Nola_Warren_SLNSW_FL19410078.jpg/500px-Nola_Warren_SLNSW_FL19410078.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Nola_Warren_SLNSW_FL19410078.jpg/500px-Nola_Warren_SLNSW_FL19410078.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Nola Warren c. 1936","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANola_Warren_SLNSW_FL19410078.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg/500px-French_-_Pendant_with_a_Monk_and_Death_-_Walters_71461.jpg?utm_source